    "turn_text": "The biggest takeaway has really been from my time spent in the Central Valley and in the Imperial Valley. When you run statewide and you spend time in parts of the state you haven't spent as much time in the past, it exposes you to a whole range of other challenges. In the Central Valley, there are thousands and thousands of people who can't drink the water. You expect to be briefed about the competition between the cities and the farms for water. You don't expect to see such prevalence of people who can't drink the water out of their faucet, the extent of asthma among young people in the Central Valley because of the air quality problems, the rural hospitals that are shutting down, the lack of broadband access. A lot of problems you associate with other parts of the country, and when it's hammered home that actually if the Central Valley were its own state, it'd be the poorest state in the union, it's pretty striking.",
